1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
 Hi and welcome back. In a previous lecture we have seen how giving MCP server a broad access to a back-end services

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
 could lead to a broken authorization vulnerabilities and in a specific to a confused duty attack.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,000
 Today we will explore another common vulnerability in AI applications which is called the prompt injection.

4
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:27,000
 We will be using a customer chatbot application to demonstrate this attack.

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:35,000
 The chatbot is powered by AI and it's supposed to help customers with the problems related to their orders.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:41,000
 For simplicity I created the chatbot application as a command line application.

7
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:46,000
 In real world the support chatbot typically would be a web-based application.

8
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:56,000
 Also to keep things simple in the chatbot application source code I heart-coded the default customer ID to be customer number one.

9
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:04,000
 This is for simplicity. In real world again the customer identity is identified through authentication mechanisms.

10
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:12,000
 The whole idea here is to focus about the prompt injection attack and avoid complexity with implementing authentication.

11
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:20,000
 Let's go with the data flow a little bit. So the chatbot takes the customers' questions and then it append them to a prompt template.

12
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:26,000
 Then it sends the prompt to an AI model hosted in LM Studio.

13
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:34,000
 We are using LM Studio here for simplicity and real world companies would probably be using enterprise grade AI models.

14
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:43,000
 The prompt itself instructs the AI model to generate an SQL query based on the customer input or customer question.

15
00:01:43,000 --> 00:01:51,000
 The SQL query is supposed to query the back-end database in order to get information about the customer's orders.

16
00:01:51,000 --> 00:02:01,000
 So once the chatbot gets the SQL query from the AI model it pass this SQL query to an MCP server tool called search database.

17
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:06,000
 The search database is going to execute the SQL query against the back-end database.

18
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:18,000
 And once we have the response from the database it's passed again to AI model in order to generate a human friendly response which is supposed to help customers with their problems.

19
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:28,000
 Now notice here that the SQL query itself it was generated by the AI model based on a prompt that contains customer's input.

20
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:36,000
 And just like any security vulnerability anything built on user input can lead to a security problems.

21
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:41,000
 Let's have a look at the code snippet that builds the prompt from the user input.

22
00:02:41,000 --> 00:02:48,000
 If you open the chatbot up script file and navigate to generate SQL with LLM method here.

23
00:02:48,000 --> 00:02:52,000
 We can see the prompt template defined under this method.

24
00:02:52,000 --> 00:02:58,000
 The prompt instructs the AI model to help in generating a Postgres SQL statement.

25
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,000
 It asks to only retire SQL statement without any explanation, back-text or comments.

26
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:10,000
 We can also see that it hardcoded the user identity to be customer one.

27
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:12,000
 And this is the part that I said before.

28
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 For demonstration purposes we are hard coding the customer identity in a set of building you know like authentication logic.

29
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:27,000
 Where in real-world the customer identity should come from authentication session like cookie or authorization token.

30
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:36,000
 We can also see that the prompt provides the database schema to the AI model so it can know which tables and columns exist.

31
00:03:36,000 --> 00:03:40,000
 Finally the prompt appends the user query here.

32
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:43,000
 Let's see this chatbot in action and play with it a little bit.

33
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:51,000
 So I have my Docker compose up and running which will start the MCP server as well as the Postgres database.

34
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:58,000
 I also have the LLM Studio running locally on port 1, 2, 3, 4 and all what I need to do is to start the chatbot script.

35
00:03:58,000 --> 00:04:02,000
 And as soon as I do that we should see some logs in the LLM Studio.

36
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:12,000
 So let me go ahead and start this and to verify we should see some logs in the LLM Studio part and this should be under the developer tab.

37
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:17,000
 So the chatbot starts with sample equations that a customer might ask.

38
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:24,000
 I'm going to copy and paste one of those questions so let's go maybe with the second one to show me all my pending orders.

39
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:35,000
 And we can see that I added some verbose logging in order to see the generated SQL queries the interaction with LLM Studio with the MCP in real time.

40
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:42,000
 By the way, we should also see some real time logs on the LLM Studio part and that happens under the developer tab.

41
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,000
 Okay, back to our chatbot.

42
00:04:44,000 --> 00:04:51,000
 We can see it first connected to the LLM Studio and then the LLM Studio generated this SQL query.

43
00:04:51,000 --> 00:05:02,000
 We can see it says to select everything from order stable where the customer ID is the one that we hardcoded and a filter that says order status is pending,

44
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:05,000
 which is like a pretty basic SQL query.

45
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:11,000
 Then our chatbot passed that to the MCP server to execute this SQL.

46
00:05:11,000 --> 00:05:19,000
 It retrieved the raw result from the database and then passed those into LLM Studio in order to do some formatting.

47
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:25,000
 To map this really quick back to our diagram, the chatbot took the customer or the user query,

48
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:32,000
 appended that into a prompt template and then passed it to the LLM Studio to generate SQL query.

49
00:05:32,000 --> 00:05:40,000
 Once we have the SQL query, the chatbot passed that to the MCP server to execute that in the DB and then the response.

50
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:49,000
 Again, it passed it to LLM Studio to have to have a formatted output and then the output is passed back to the user or to the customer.

51
00:05:49,000 --> 00:05:53,000
 At this point, the chatbot is working as expected.

52
00:05:53,000 --> 00:05:59,000
 And I don't want to make this video too long, so I will leave it to you to explore the chatbot functionality further.

53
00:05:59,000 --> 00:06:03,000
 And see if you can access data that you are not authorized to access.

54
